ns so the snapshot  that we got from post day more is that  the way Google handled that situation  cause people to become less open and  less willing to discuss discuss these  issues now in a one-way that's awful  because it's chilling of speech but in  another way is it maybe a good thing  that people aren't talking about  politics in the workplace it depends on  what type of ethos the company  encourages it you know Google places  like Salesforce they promote themselves  as being environments where they want  you to bring your whole self to work  they actively encourage it they invite  speakers to talk about policy or  politically oriented topics and so if if  that is going to be the ethos and the  culture that you create in the company  then you are doing a disservice to a  percentage perhaps a significant  percentage of your population who feel  that they cannot offer their perspective  in the workplace because they're going  to be shot it down  what happens Silicon Valley is  interesting where a lot of companies and  in the greater Bay Area part of their  brand part of their identity part of  their value proposition the customers is  that we're not just another company you  you can get tech you support from any  place but we are interested in helping  you know starving villagers in China or  we are committed to gender diversity  things like that is this you know it can  can you square the circle where is it  always going to be the fact that certain  companies are going to be like hey you  know what we  are very liberal or we're a very  conservative company and we don't you  know you don't fit in here so you  shouldn't work here I mean is there an  or resolvable tension in that so there  is this interesting narrative and  Silicon Valley that these companies are  saving the world even if it's you know  swipe left swipe right somehow they're  saving the world so there's a lot there  repopulating the world well there you go  that's that's important as well so  ther